<Project>

  <!--
    The overlay worker is a sibling executable rather than a library, because
    general overlay cannot be interrupted in-process and has to be killable
    (Q-97, ADR-022 §9, benchmarks/geometry-overlay). Whoever needs to RUN it —
    the host, and the tests that prove the deadline works — needs its whole
    dependency closure in a directory beside them.

    Opt in with <CopyOverlayWorker>true</CopyOverlayWorker>. Defined here rather
    than duplicated in two project files so the two cannot drift; the first
    version lived only in the host, and every pool test failed with "the overlay
    worker is not at ..." because the test assembly runs from its own directory.
  -->
  <Target Name="CopyOverlayWorker"
          AfterTargets="Build"
          Condition="'$(CopyOverlayWorker)' == 'true'">
    <ItemGroup>

      <!--
        <b>Excluding the other sibling, and this is not defensive tidiness — it shipped.</b>
        A worker's build output can contain a stale `overlay/` or `importer/` from an earlier
        build that was pointed at it, and the copy below is recursive. Measured 2026-09-10:
        `Graticula.Overlay.Worker/bin` held a 426 MB `importer/`, and
        `Graticula.Import.Reader/bin` held an 855 MB `importer/` inside which was another
        `overlay/` — it accumulates, because each copy carries the last one.

        <b>The consequence was the opposite of what the overlay copy was for.</b> Carrying
        the overlay worker into the image is free precisely because it has no GDAL; carrying
        a stale `importer/` nested inside it puts GDAL in by the back door, silently, which
        is what [ADR-016](docs/adr/ADR-016-packaging-deployment-upgrade.md) §2's claim was
        about. A first measurement showed 12 files and a later one 426 MB, from the same
        target against the same project.
      -->
      <OverlayWorkerFiles
        Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)src\Graticula.Overlay.Worker\bin\$(Configuration)\$(TargetFramework)\**\*"
        Exclude="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)src\Graticula.Overlay.Worker\bin\$(Configuration)\$(TargetFramework)\**\overlay\**\*;$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)src\Graticula.Overlay.Worker\bin\$(Configuration)\$(TargetFramework)\**\importer\**\*" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <Copy SourceFiles="@(OverlayWorkerFiles)"
          DestinationFolder="$(OutDir)overlay\%(RecursiveDir)"
          SkipUnchangedFiles="true" />
  </Target>

  <!--
    The geodatabase reader is the same arrangement for a different reason. The overlay worker is a
    separate process because OverlayNG cannot be interrupted; this one is because GDAL parses a file
    somebody else chose, and ADR-009 §2.2's words for keeping it out of the serving process are that it
    "removes an untrusted-file parser from the process that serves public requests" (ADR-037 §5a, D-88).

    Opt in with <CopyImportReader>true</CopyImportReader>.

    <b>It is much larger than the overlay worker and that is worth knowing before you wait for it.</b>
    MaxRev.Gdal's Windows and Linux runtime payloads are both restored, so the directory copied here
    carries two platforms' native libraries. SkipUnchangedFiles makes the second build cheap; the first
    is not, and a clean build of anything that opts in pays it.
  -->
  <Target Name="CopyImportReader"
          AfterTargets="Build"
          Condition="'$(CopyImportReader)' == 'true'">
    <ItemGroup>
      <ImportReaderFiles
        Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)src\Graticula.Import.Reader\bin\$(Configuration)\$(TargetFramework)\**\*"
        Exclude="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)src\Graticula.Import.Reader\bin\$(Configuration)\$(TargetFramework)\**\overlay\**\*;$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)src\Graticula.Import.Reader\bin\$(Configuration)\$(TargetFramework)\**\importer\**\*" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <Copy SourceFiles="@(ImportReaderFiles)"
          DestinationFolder="$(OutDir)importer\%(RecursiveDir)"
          SkipUnchangedFiles="true" />
  </Target>

  <!--
    <b>And the same for `publish`, which was the whole of [D-235](docs/architecture-debt.md)'s
    overlay half.</b> Both targets above run `AfterTargets="Build"` and copy into `$(OutDir)`.
    A published output is assembled from known content items into `$(PublishDir)`, so files
    dropped into `$(OutDir)` after the build are not in it — measured 2026-09-09: a publish of
    the host produced 49 entries and neither `overlay/` nor `importer/`.

    <b>The gap appeared exclusively in the artefact nobody runs locally</b>, which is why it
    survived a release. `dotnet build` puts both siblings in place, so every developer machine
    and every test run has them; only the image does not, and only the image ships.

    <b>Only the overlay worker is carried here, and the asymmetry is the point.</b> It is
    NetTopologySuite and nothing else — no native payload, no GDAL — so putting it in the
    serving image costs nothing that [ADR-016](docs/adr/ADR-016-packaging-deployment-upgrade.md)
    §2's *No GDAL, no Python* row claims as an attack-surface argument. The import reader
    carries GDAL's native runtimes for two platforms, so carrying it is exactly the trade that
    row is about, and that is ADR-016's decision rather than a line to add here. Doing the free
    half now is not deferring the other one: it restores every GeometryServer overlay operation
    to every shipped image, which is a v1 capability that no published artefact could perform.
  -->
  <Target Name="PublishOverlayWorker"
          AfterTargets="Publish"
          Condition="'$(CopyOverlayWorker)' == 'true'">
    <ItemGroup>
      <PublishedOverlayWorkerFiles
        Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)src\Graticula.Overlay.Worker\bin\$(Configuration)\$(TargetFramework)\**\*"
        Exclude="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)src\Graticula.Overlay.Worker\bin\$(Configuration)\$(TargetFramework)\**\overlay\**\*;$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)src\Graticula.Overlay.Worker\bin\$(Configuration)\$(TargetFramework)\**\importer\**\*" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <Error Condition="'@(PublishedOverlayWorkerFiles)' == ''"
           Text="The overlay worker's build output is empty, so this publish would produce an image whose GeometryServer cannot answer a single overlay operation. That is D-235 and it is not something to warn about." />

    <Copy SourceFiles="@(PublishedOverlayWorkerFiles)"
          DestinationFolder="$(PublishDir)overlay\%(RecursiveDir)"
          SkipUnchangedFiles="true" />
  </Target>

  <!--
    <b>And the import reader, by owner decision 2026-09-10 —
    [D-235](docs/architecture-debt.md).</b> This one was held back when the overlay worker
    went in, because it is the half that costs something: it carries GDAL's native runtimes,
    and [ADR-016](docs/adr/ADR-016-packaging-deployment-upgrade.md) §2's server row claimed
    *No GDAL, no Python* as an attack-surface argument. Asked where GDAL should live, the
    owner chose the serving image — so that claim is struck in ADR-016 rather than quietly
    contradicted here, and [A-016](docs/architecture-assumptions.md) goes with it.

    <b>What was traded, in one sentence.</b> A published image can now import a geodatabase
    and a shapefile, which v1 scope calls important and which no shipped artefact could do;
    and the process that serves public requests now has GDAL's libraries on disk beside it.
    **It still does not load them** — the reader is a separate process and
    `NativeDependencyTests` checks that in both directions, including through project
    references — so what changed is the image's contents rather than the serving process's
    address space. That distinction is the whole of what is left of the original argument,
    and ADR-016 says so rather than pretending nothing moved.
  -->
  <Target Name="PublishImportReader"
          AfterTargets="Publish"
          Condition="'$(CopyImportReader)' == 'true'">
    <ItemGroup>
      <PublishedImportReaderFiles
        Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)src\Graticula.Import.Reader\bin\$(Configuration)\$(TargetFramework)\**\*"
        Exclude="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)src\Graticula.Import.Reader\bin\$(Configuration)\$(TargetFramework)\**\overlay\**\*;$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)src\Graticula.Import.Reader\bin\$(Configuration)\$(TargetFramework)\**\importer\**\*" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <Error Condition="'@(PublishedImportReaderFiles)' == ''"
           Text="The import reader's build output is empty, so this publish would produce an image that cannot import a geodatabase or a shapefile. That is D-235 and it is not something to warn about." />

    <Copy SourceFiles="@(PublishedImportReaderFiles)"
          DestinationFolder="$(PublishDir)importer\%(RecursiveDir)"
          SkipUnchangedFiles="true" />
  </Target>

</Project>
